<GuidePage
  slug="how-to-run-a-fair-giveaway"
  description="How to run a fair Instagram or YouTube giveaway: choose a prize, write clear rules, collect entries, pick a random winner openly, contact the winner and avoid fake accounts and scams."
  intro="A well-run giveaway grows your audience and builds trust. A badly run one, with vague rules, a winner nobody can verify or a prize that never arrives, does the opposite. This guide walks through doing it properly."
>
  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Why fairness matters</h2>
    <p>
      Every giveaway comment section has someone asking “Was it rigged?” Your audience can’t see how you picked the
      winner, so they judge by what they can see: clear rules, a transparent draw, and a winner who really exists and
      really gets the prize. Get those right and people will happily enter your next one. Get them wrong and the
      giveaway can cost you followers.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Step 1: Choose the right prize</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Match the prize to your audience.</strong> A baking channel giving away a stand mixer attracts bakers. A
        generic phone giveaway attracts people who only want a free phone and will unfollow the day after.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Give products or experiences, not cash.</strong> Cash prizes attract fake accounts and bots, and in some
        places they bring extra legal rules.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Know the full cost.</strong> Include shipping, packaging and any taxes. If you can only ship within your
        country, say so in the rules.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Be clear about sponsored prizes.</strong> If a brand provided the prize, say so. Most platforms and
        advertising rules expect sponsorships to be disclosed.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Step 2: Write clear rules</h2>
    <p>
      Rules protect you as much as your entrants. Post them in the caption or description, or link to them, before the
      giveaway starts. Good rules answer these questions:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Who can enter?</strong> Minimum age, countries or regions, and whether employees or family can join.</li>
      <li><strong>How do you enter?</strong> For example, “Comment with the name of a friend who’d love this.”</li>
      <li><strong>How many entries per person?</strong> One per person, or one per comment? Can tagging more friends add entries?</li>
      <li><strong>When does it close?</strong> Give an exact date, time and time zone.</li>
      <li><strong>How is the winner picked?</strong> “A random draw from all valid entries.”</li>
      <li><strong>How and when will the winner be told?</strong> And how long do they have to reply before you pick a backup?</li>
      <li><strong>What exactly is the prize?</strong> Model, colour, quantity, and whether it can be swapped.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="tip">
      <strong>Keep entry free.</strong> Don’t require a purchase, a payment or a fee to enter. In many countries, India
      included, a draw that needs payment to enter can count as a lottery, which is tightly regulated. Platforms also
      have their own promotion rules, so read the current guidelines for Instagram, YouTube or wherever you post.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Step 3: Promote it and watch for fakes</h2>
    <p>
      Pin the giveaway post, share it in stories or a community post, and remind people a day before it closes. While
      it runs, keep an eye out for:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Bot accounts</strong> with no profile picture, no posts and random usernames, leaving dozens of comments.</li>
      <li><strong>Impersonators</strong> copying your name and photo, then messaging entrants to say they “won” and asking for a fee.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Warn your audience in the post: <em>“We will never ask you for money, card details or a password. The winner will
      only be announced from this account.”</em> It takes one line and protects your followers from scams.
    </p>
  </section>

  <AdSlot name="inGuide" />

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Step 4: Collect and clean the entries</h2>
    <p>
      When the giveaway closes, gather every entry into a single list, one name per line. You can copy usernames from
      the comments by hand, or export them with a comment export tool you trust. Then clean the list:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Remove entries that break the rules</strong>, such as comments after the closing time, entries from
        outside the allowed region, or your own account.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Apply your entry limit.</strong> If it is one entry per person, remove duplicate usernames. If each
        comment counts, keep the duplicates.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Remove obvious bots</strong>, using the same rule for everyone.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Write down how many valid entries you have. Sharing that number makes the draw more believable.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Step 5: Pick the winner openly</h2>
    <p>
      Use a random picker rather than scrolling and choosing, and let your audience see it happen. Our{' '}
      <a href="/giveaway-winner-picker/">giveaway winner picker</a> works from a pasted list, so you never connect your
      Instagram or YouTube account to anything. It can count each name once (or keep repeat comments as extra entries),
      leave out accounts such as your own, pick several winners and backups, and shows “Picked on winisland.in” on the
      result.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Start a screen recording or go live.</li>
      <li>Show your entry list and the number of entries.</li>
      <li>Paste the list into the picker, set the number of winners and backups, and press pick.</li>
      <li>Keep the result on screen and take a screenshot.</li>
      <li>Share the recording or screenshot when you announce the winner.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Pick <strong>one or two backup winners</strong> at the same time. If the first winner doesn’t reply in time,
      you move to the next name from the same recorded draw instead of running a new one that people could question.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Step 6: Contact the winner and deliver the prize</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Announce publicly first</strong>, in a story, a reply to the winning comment or an updated caption. Then
        message the winner from your verified, official account.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Ask only for what you need</strong> to send the prize: usually a name, a phone number for delivery and an
        address. Never ask for payment, bank details or passwords.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Give a reply deadline</strong>, such as 48 hours, as stated in your rules.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Ship with tracking</strong> and keep the receipt. Ask the winner, politely, if they would be happy to share
        a photo when it arrives. It is the best proof your giveaways are real.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Delete their personal details</strong> once the prize has arrived.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <h2>Giveaway rules template</h2>
    <p>Adapt this to your own giveaway. Keep it short so people actually read it.</p>
    <div class="template">
      <p><strong>🎁 GIVEAWAY: [prize]</strong></p>
      <p>
        To enter: follow @[account] and comment with [entry action]. One entry per person. Open to residents of [country],
        aged 18+. Closes [date, time, time zone].
      </p>
      <p>
        One winner and two backups will be picked at random from all valid entries and announced on this account
        within [number] days. The winner has [hours] hours to reply before the prize goes to a backup.
      </p>
      <p>
        No purchase necessary. This giveaway is not sponsored, endorsed or run by [platform]. We will never ask for money
        or passwords.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel faq">
    <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <details>
      <summary>Do “tag three friends” giveaways work?</summary>
      <p>
        They bring lots of comments, but many tagged friends never engage again. Asking a question related to the prize,
        like “What would you bake first?”, attracts more genuine followers.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>How long should a giveaway run?</summary>
      <p>
        Three to seven days suits most accounts. Shorter giveaways build urgency; longer ones give more people a chance to
        see the post.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Can I pick a winner without connecting my account?</summary>
      <p>
        Yes. Copy the entries into a list and paste them into a winner picker that works in your browser, like ours. No
        login or account access is needed.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>What if the winner turns out to be a fake account?</summary>
      <p>
        Disqualify them under your rules and move to the first backup winner from the same draw. Say so publicly to keep
        things transparent.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>
</GuidePage>

